<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#demotic-egyptian-script">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demotic Egyptian script</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 650 BCE–452 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Demotic, Enchorial Egyptian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demotic is the ancient Egyptian script derived from northern forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieratic">hieratic</a> used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, named 'popular' by the Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> called it 'document writing'. Written from right to left, it developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> late in the Twenty-fifth Dynasty and became the official administrative and legal script under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasis-ii">Amasis II</a>. Used first for administrative, legal and commercial texts, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> it also served literature and religion, then declined in public life under Roman rule. The last dated example is a graffito at the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> from 452 CE. Its 32 lines on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a> were deciphered before the hieroglyphs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demotic-egyptian-script.jpg" alt="Demotic Egyptian script" width="330" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris 73 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DemoticScriptsRosettaStoneReplica.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Coptic, Egyptian written in Greek letters with several signs taken from <b>Demotic</b>, became the language of a new literature and is still used in the liturgy of the Coptic Orthodox Church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…about 2700 to 2000 BCE; Middle Egyptian, the classical language of the Middle Kingdom, used for inscriptions until the end of antiquity; Late Egyptian, the language of the New Kingdom from about 1350 BCE; <b>Demotic</b>, from the 7th century BCE; and Coptic, written from the 3rd century CE.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>It belongs to the Later Egyptian phase, the colloquial speech of the later periods, and its core vocabulary is closest to the <b>Demotic</b> phase before it.</blockquote><a href="/language/coptic">Coptic · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings called the priests of all Egypt to synods, at Canopus in 238 BCE and at Memphis in 217 BCE; the decree of 217 BCE, in hieroglyphs, <b>Demotic</b> and Greek, presented the king as Horus and set up his statue in every temple.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Art and texts deliberately revived Old Kingdom models, and the <b>Demotic</b> script came into use.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
